EU ministers of Agriculture will have a  second debate on a regulation concerning  promotion measures for agricultural  products on the internal market and in  third countries the regulation proposes  to better inform consumers and other  stakeholders on the high quality of food  produced in Europe for strengthening its  competitiveness and develop new markets  ministers will also discuss the  commission's report on the possibility  to introduce mandatory indication of the  country of origin for meat used as an  ingredient the report addresses the  feasibility costs and benefits of  providing such information on food  labels including the legal impact on the  internal market and on international  trade the conclusions of this report  were expected after last year's float on  beef meat which affected several Member  States furthermore ministers will be  briefed by the Commission on a proposal  for a regulation on organic production  and labeling which aims to renew and  simplify the existing legal framework  later on the day fisheries ministers  will take note of the outcome of the  coastal states consultations on mackerel  and will have the opportunity to  exchange views on the subject the  coastal states mackerel arrangement  involving the EU Norway the Faroe  Islands and Iceland allows for a shared  management approach promoting a  sustainable fishing of this stock it has  not been applied since 2010 but  negotiations have been fruitful between  the EU and some other coastal states
 

